Subject: [PATCH] mali_kbase: wait for devfreq resume work on system resume

kbase_device_suspend() enqueues DEVFREQ_WORK_SUSPEND and then calls
flush_workqueue() to block until it actually runs. kbase_device_resume()
enqueues the mirror-image DEVFREQ_WORK_RESUME but never waits for it -
system resume (and rendering) can complete before the queued work item
gets around to calling devfreq_resume_device(), which is what restarts
the GPU's DVFS governor polling. Until that catches up, the GPU can be
left at whatever (low) frequency it was at across suspend while already
being asked to render - observed as an FPS drop right after suspend/
resume from freeze/idle that self-corrects once the governor's next
poll fires.

Make resume symmetric with suspend by flushing the workqueue before
returning.

---
--- a/product/kernel/drivers/gpu/arm/midgard/mali_kbase_core_linux.c
+++ b/product/kernel/drivers/gpu/arm/midgard/mali_kbase_core_linux.c
@@ -5976,8 +5976,20 @@
 
 #ifdef CONFIG_MALI_DEVFREQ
 	dev_dbg(dev, "Callback %s\n", __func__);
-	if (kbdev->devfreq)
+	if (kbdev->devfreq) {
 		kbase_devfreq_enqueue_work(kbdev, DEVFREQ_WORK_RESUME);
+		/*
+		 * Unlike kbase_device_suspend() above, this used to return
+		 * without waiting for the enqueued DEVFREQ_WORK_RESUME to
+		 * actually run devfreq_resume_device(). System resume (and
+		 * therefore rendering) could complete before the GPU's DVFS
+		 * governor was told to resume polling, leaving the GPU at
+		 * whatever low frequency it was left at across suspend -
+		 * seen as a real FPS drop right after suspend/resume from
+		 * freeze/idle until the governor caught up on its own.
+		 */
+		flush_workqueue(kbdev->devfreq_queue.workq);
+	}
 #endif
 	return 0;
 }
